  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 1255 AM CDT Wed Sep 2 2026\n\nDangerous hot temperatures 10-15 degrees above normal will remain in \nplace through Saturday with an Extreme Heat Warning in effect for \ndaily heat index values of 103-109 degrees. The stout 594 dm ridge is \nnow peaking over the FA with daily maxTs maxing out in the \nupper 90s to 100 degrees locally. Extreme heat impacts remain \nmore probable into the start of the holiday weekend as dewpoints\nhave struggled to mix down into the upper 60s. This is due to a\nresidual evapotranspiration in locations that saw recent heavy \nrainfall, while even locations that did achieve lower values \nstill managed to near warning criteria. Given that the deep dry \nadiabatic soundings and robust mixing will persist, nudged maxTs\nup in our southern counties with CONSShort the next two days as\nthey have been running cool on the NBM despite dewpoints \nremaining near 70. Would expect MaxTs to reach their peak by \nThursday as we will no longer be mixing down anomalous 700 mb \nwarm air aloft, while there will also be a subtle increase in \ndewpoint. \n\nEven if some locations do not quite reach the 4 day criteria of 105, \nthe extreme impacts are still going to be there. This is when \nyou factor in consecutive record warm overnight low temperatures\nin the mid-70s, and a longer duration heatwave that is now \nprogged to persist through at least Saturday. Although there is \na 20% chance of an isolated shower or thunderstorm on Saturday \nmainly northeast over southwest Indiana and portions of southern\nIllinois and western Kentucky, it will not be enough to provide\nrelief. Both the GFS/ECMWF do remain in better agreement with \nthe ridge breaking down on Saturday that will send a shortwave \ndownstream. There is a 20-30% chance of rainfall exceeding a \ntenth of an inch where isolated to scattered showers and \nthunderstorms occur. Similar to Friday, the greatest \nprobabilities will be focused northeast.\n\nBy Sunday, the current consensus is for a backdoor cold front to \npush southwest as the ridge retreats, but it doesn't quite reach the \nMississippi. The probability on the LREF is 40-60% for needing to \nextend advisory level heat headlines for heat index values \nremaining at or above 100 degrees. The highest confidence is \nover SEMO, but uncertainty is high on where you drawl the cutoff\nfurther northeast as it depends on the location of the front. \nMaxTs only cool off a few degrees into the mid 90s, with lower \n90s more favored for Labor Day as the heatwave finally comes to \nan end. The probability of high temperatures remaining below 95\ndegrees on the NBM is 70-90%.",
